{"metadata":{"citation_count_without_self_citations":289,"citation_count":332,"publication_info":[{"year":2018,"artid":"031103","journal_issue":"3","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214495"},"journal_volume":"120"}],"core":true,"dois":[{"value":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.120.031103"}],"titles":[{"title":"Quasinormal modes and Strong Cosmic Censorship","source":"APS"},{"title":"Quasinormal modes and Strong Cosmic Censorship","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"b707b48c-a123-478a-b2ad-a7b2503d4777","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1020444"},"full_name":"Cardoso, Vitor","affiliations":[{"value":"Lisbon, CENTRA","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/908999"}},{"value":"Perimeter Inst. Theor. Phys.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/908474"}}],"signature_block":"CARDASv","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"CENTRA, Departamento de Física, Instituto Superior Técnico—IST, Universidade de Lisboa—UL , Avenida Rovisco Pais 1, 1049 Lisboa, Portugal"},{"value":"Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics , 31 Caroline Street North Waterloo, Ontario N2L 2Y5, Canada"}]},{"uuid":"7ec28c18-7eee-43ac-bfca-7c6b94fd1d2c","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1056096"},"full_name":"Costa, João L.","affiliations":[{"value":"Lisbon, CFTP","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/911028"}},{"value":"Lisbon, IST Maths","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/910023"}}],"signature_block":"CASTj","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Departamento de Matemática, ISCTE—Instituto Universitário de Lisboa , Av. Forças Armadas 1649-026 Lisboa, Portugal"},{"value":"Center for Mathematical Analysis, Geometry and Dynamical Systems, Instituto Superior Técnico—IST, Universidade de Lisboa—UL , Avenida Rovisco Pais 1, 1049 Lisboa, Portugal"}]},{"uuid":"abdac2e8-5335-4036-a4bf-02bcd4af623b","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1719058"},"full_name":"Destounis, Kyriakos","affiliations":[{"value":"Lisbon, CENTRA","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/908999"}}],"signature_block":"DASTANk","curated_relation":true,"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"CENTRA, Departamento de Física, Instituto Superior Técnico—IST, Universidade de Lisboa—UL , Avenida Rovisco Pais 1, 1049 Lisboa, Portugal"}]},{"uuid":"b076f19d-1481-4563-9015-465370388f4f","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1272295"},"full_name":"Hintz, Peter","affiliations":[{"value":"UC, Berkeley, Math. Dept.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903300"}}],"signature_block":"HADp","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics, University of California , Berkeley, California 94720-3840, USA"}]},{"uuid":"9f855874-c72e-4bc8-881f-39cf46b09893","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1316434"},"full_name":"Jansen, Aron","affiliations":[{"value":"Utrecht U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903317"}}],"signature_block":"JANSANa","curated_relation":true,"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Institute for Theoretical Physics and Center for Extreme Matter and Emergent Phenomena, Utrecht University , 3508 TD Utrecht, The Netherlands"}]}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"0ecbf70897f27dc1fec323d82fe20f6f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0ecbf70897f27dc1fec323d82fe20f6f","source":"arxiv","caption":"Lowest lying quasinormal modes for $l=1$ and $\\Lambda M^2 = 0.06$ (left) and $0.14$ (right), as a function of $Q/M$. The top plots show the imaginary part, with dashed red lines corresponding to purely imaginary modes, and solid blue to complex, ``PS'' modes, whose real part is shown in the lower plots. The red circles in the top plots indicate the modes of empty de Sitter at the same $\\Lambda$, which closely matches the first imaginary mode here, but lie increasingly less close to the higher modes. Near the extremal limit of maximal charge, another set of purely imaginary modes (dotted green lines) comes in from $-\\infty$ and approaches $0$ in the limit. Only a finite number of modes are shown, even though we expect infinitely many complex and extremal modes in the range shown.","filename":"Figure_constantLslices.png"},{"key":"07cb8c1dad4b0ccffb275b17f4335b4c","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/07cb8c1dad4b0ccffb275b17f4335b4c","source":"arxiv","caption":"Parameter space of the RNdS solutions, bounded by a line of extremal solutions of maximal charge where $r_- = r_+$ on top, and for $\\Lambda M^2 &gt; 1/9$ a line of extremal solutions where $r_c = r_+$. In the physical region the value of $\\beta$ is shown. For small $\\Lambda M^2$ the dominant mode is the $l=1$ de Sitter mode, shown in shades of red. For larger $\\Lambda M^2$ the dominant mode is the large $l$ complex, PS mode, here showing the $l=100$ WKB approximated mode in shades of blue. For very large $Q/M$ the $l=0$ extremal mode dominates. The green sliver on top where the NE mode dominates is merely indicative, the true numerical region is too small to be noticeable on these scales.","filename":"Figure_ContourPlot.png"},{"key":"312e082a942418b3719599bc92517b35","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/312e082a942418b3719599bc92517b35","source":"arxiv","caption":"Dominant modes of different types, showing the (nearly) dominant complex PS mode (blue, solid) at $l= 10$, the dominant de Sitter mode (red, dotted) at $l=1$ and the dominant NE mode (green, dashed) at $l=0$. The two dashed vertical lines indicate the points where $\\beta \\equiv -\\operatorname{Im}(\\omega)/\\kappa_- =1/2$ and where the NE becomes dominant. (Note that the value of $\\beta$ is only relevant for $\\Lambda&gt;0$.)","filename":"Figure_nearExtremal.png"},{"key":"6bcf8eaca4d197258167d5bd391d208d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6bcf8eaca4d197258167d5bd391d208d","source":"arxiv","caption":"Scalar wavefunctions $\\phi(x)$ (defined in Eq.~(\\ref{phidef}), with $x = (r - r_+)/(r_c - r_+)$) of the dominant mode of each of the three families, at $\\Lambda M^2 = 0.02$ and $Q/Q_{\\text{max}} \\approx 0.9966$, as indicated in the top right of Fig. (\\ref{nearExtremalModes}). Shown are the near extremal mode with $\\omega_{\\text{NE}}/\\kappa_- = -0.87005i$ (for $l=0$, green dotted line), the dS mode with $\\omega_{\\text{dS}}/\\kappa_- = -0.87043i$ (for $l=1$, red dashed line), and the complex mode with $\\omega_{\\text{C}}/\\kappa_- = 26.448 -0.89101i$ (for $l=10$, blue solid and dash-dotted line for real and imaginary parts). The solid vertical black line indicates the light ring.","filename":"Figure_Eigenfunctions.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Cardoso:2017soq"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2018-01-18"}],"keywords":[{"value":"black hole: quasinormal mode","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"perturbation: scalar","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"black hole: charge","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"field theory: scalar","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"photon: sphere","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"horizon","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"cosmic censorship","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"space-time","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"fluctuation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"decay rate","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"de Sitter","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"The fate of Cauchy horizons, such as those found inside charged black holes, is intrinsically connected to the decay of small perturbations exterior to the event horizon. As such, the validity of the strong cosmic censorship (SCC) conjecture is tied to how effectively the exterior damps fluctuations. Here, we study massless scalar fields in the exterior of Reissner–Nordström–de Sitter black holes. Their decay rates are governed by quasinormal modes of the black hole. We identify three families of modes in these spacetimes: one directly linked to the photon sphere, well described by standard WKB-type tools; another family whose existence and time scale is closely related to the de Sitter horizon; finally, a third family which dominates for near-extremally charged black holes and which is also present in asymptotically flat spacetimes. The last two families of modes seem to have gone unnoticed in the literature. We give a detailed description of linear scalar perturbations of such black holes, and conjecture that SCC is violated in the near extremal regime.","source":"APS"},{"value":"The fate of Cauchy horizons, such as those found inside charged black holes, is intrinsically connected to the decay of small perturbations exterior to the event horizon. As such, the validity of the strong cosmic censorship (SCC) conjecture is tied to how effectively the exterior damps fluctuations. Here, we study massless scalar fields in the exterior of Reissner--Nordstrom--de Sitter black holes. Their decay rates are governed by quasinormal modes of the black hole. We identify three families of modes in these spacetimes: one directly linked to the photon sphere, well described by standard WKB-type tools; another family whose existence and timescale is closely related to the de Sitter horizon. Finally, a third family which dominates for near-extremally-charged black holes and which is also present in asymptotically flat spacetimes. The last two families of modes seem to have gone unnoticed in the literature. We give a detailed description of linear scalar perturbations of such black holes, and conjecture that SCC is violated in the near extremal regime.","source":"arXiv"}],"copyright":[{"year":2018,"holder":"American Physical Society","material":"publication"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/67635"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.5.2419"],"label":"1","authors":[{"full_name":"Price, R.H."}],"publication_info":{"year":1972,"artid":"2419","page_start":"2419","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"5"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1282894"},"reference":{"label":"2","authors":[{"full_name":"Dafermos, M."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."},{"full_name":"Shlapentokh-Rothman, Y."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1402.7034"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1501916"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1016/j.aim.2017.10.027"],"label":"3","authors":[{"full_name":"Angelopoulos, Y."},{"full_name":"Aretakis, S."},{"full_name":"Gajic, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":2018,"artid":"529","page_start":"529","journal_title":"Adv.Math.","journal_volume":"323"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/301259"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.41.1796"],"label":"4","authors":[{"full_name":"Poisson, E."},{"full_name":"Israel, W."}],"publication_info":{"year":1990,"artid":"1796","page_start":"1796","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"41"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/622602"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1002/cpa.20071"],"label":"5","authors":[{"full_name":"Dafermos, M."}],"publication_info":{"year":2005,"artid":"0445","page_start":"0445","journal_title":"Commun.Pure Appl.Math.","journal_volume":"58"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1083825"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s00220-014-2063-4"],"label":"6","authors":[{"full_name":"Dafermos, M."}],"publication_info":{"year":2014,"artid":"729","page_start":"729","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"332"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1514231"},"reference":{"label":"7","authors":[{"full_name":"Luk, J."},{"full_name":"Oh, S.-J."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1702.05715"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1514232"},"reference":{"label":"8","authors":[{"full_name":"Luk, J."},{"full_name":"Oh, S.-J."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1702.05716"},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"dois":["10.4310/MRL.1997.v4.n1.a10"],"label":"9","authors":[{"full_name":"Barreto, A. Sá"},{"full_name":"Zworski, M."}],"publication_info":{"year":1997,"artid":"103","page_start":"103","journal_title":"Math.Res.Lett.","journal_volume":"4"}}},{"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s00220-008-0553-y"],"label":"10","authors":[{"full_name":"Bony, J.-F."},{"full_name":"Häfner, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":2008,"artid":"697","page_start":"697","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"282"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/883484"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s00023-012-0159-y"],"label":"11","authors":[{"full_name":"Dyatlov, S."}],"publication_info":{"year":2012,"artid":"1101","page_start":"1101","journal_title":"Ann.Henri Poincare","journal_volume":"13"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1232381"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s00220-014-2255-y"],"label":"12","authors":[{"full_name":"Dyatlov, S."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015,"artid":"1445","page_start":"1445","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"335"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1469117"},"reference":{"label":"13","authors":[{"full_name":"Hintz, P."},{"full_name":"Vasy, A."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1606.04014"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1503384"},"reference":{"label":"14","authors":[{"full_name":"Hintz, P."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1612.04489"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/524296"},"reference":{"dois":["10.1103/PhysRevD.61.064016"],"label":"15","authors":[{"full_name":"Ori, A."}],"publication_info":{"year":2000,"artid":"064016","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"61"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1303536"},"reference":{"